  const l29 = [
    M("Hayden White and history as narrative", [
      ["Stories in the archive", "📖", "<b>Hayden White</b> (1928-2018) wrote <i>Metahistory</i> (1973). He argued that historians do not just find facts; they arrange them into a story with a beginning, middle and end. He said the shape of that story is often one of a few types: <b>romance, tragedy, comedy or satire</b>."],
      ["The linguistic turn", "🗣️", "White's work was part of the 'linguistic turn', which stressed how language and writing shape meaning. He noted that the same events could be told as a tragedy or a comedy depending on which are stressed and how they are linked."],
      ["Pushback", "⚖️", "Critics said this blurs history and fiction. White replied that facts still limit what can be said: you cannot write that a battle never happened. Many historians now accept that narrative choices matter, while insisting that evidence must support the account."]],
      ["Think about how a historian turns facts into a story.", "White's key idea concerns the shape or plot of an account, not inventing facts.", "Example: a school sports season could be told as a comeback story or as a story of lost chances, using the same scores. Now do yours."],
      [["Hayden White wrote...", "Metahistory", "The Histories|The Prince"],
       ["White argued that historians arrange facts into...", "Narrative stories", "Tables only|Poems only"],
       ["Which is one of White's story types?", "Tragedy", "Mystery|Western"],
       ["The 'linguistic turn' stressed the role of...", "Language", "Rainfall|Distance"],
       ["Critics worried that White's view blurs history and...", "Fiction", "Geography|Math"],
       ["White agreed that facts still...", "Limit what can be said", "Do not matter|Change every day"],
       ["The same events can be told as a tragedy or a comedy depending on...", "Emphasis and linking", "The weather|The font used"],
       ["Many historians today accept that narrative choices matter but also insist on...", "Evidence", "Imagination alone|Secrecy"]]),
    M("Foucault: power, knowledge and discourse", [
      ["A French thinker", "🔎", "<b>Michel Foucault</b> (1926-1984) was a French philosopher and historian of ideas. In <i>Madness and Civilization</i> (1961) and <i>Discipline and Punish</i> (1975) he studied how societies treat the mentally ill, criminals and the sick, and how institutions like asylums, hospitals and prisons shape people."],
      ["Power and knowledge", "🔗", "Foucault argued that <b>knowledge</b> and <b>power</b> are connected: those who define what is 'normal' or 'true' gain power over others. A <b>discourse</b> is a way of speaking and thinking about a subject that sets what can be said. His method of tracing how ideas and institutions came about is called <b>genealogy</b>."],
      ["The Panopticon", "👁️", "Foucault used Jeremy Bentham's <b>Panopticon</b> (a prison design from the 1780s-90s with a central watchtower) as an image of modern discipline: people behave because they might be watched. Critics say he sometimes generalized from limited evidence, but historians still use his questions."]],
      ["Think about the link Foucault saw between what people know and who has power.", "A discourse is a way of talking about a subject; genealogy traces how it came to be.", "Example: school grades are a way of defining 'good' students, which shapes how they are treated. Now do yours."],
      [["Foucault was a...", "French philosopher", "Roman general|Russian czar"],
       ["Foucault wrote Discipline and...", "Punish", "Power|Progress"],
       ["Foucault linked knowledge to...", "Power", "Weather|Money only"],
       ["A discourse is a way of...", "Speaking and thinking about a subject", "Building bridges|Counting votes"],
       ["His method of tracing how ideas and institutions came about is called...", "Genealogy", "Geometry|Cartography"],
       ["Who designed the Panopticon prison?", "Jeremy Bentham", "Karl Marx|Isaac Newton"],
       ["The Panopticon image shows people behaving because they...", "Might be watched", "Are asleep|Have no rules"],
       ["A criticism of Foucault is that he sometimes...", "Generalized from limited evidence", "Used no books|Wrote only in verse"]]),
    M("World-systems theory and dependency", [
      ["Core and periphery", "🌐", "Sociologist <b>Immanuel Wallerstein</b> wrote <i>The Modern World-System</i> (1974). He argued that from about the 1500s a capitalist world economy linked regions in unequal roles: a wealthy <b>core</b> (like northwestern Europe), a <b>periphery</b> (supplying raw goods and labor) and a <b>semi-periphery</b> in between."],
      ["Dependency ideas", "📉", "In the 1950s-60s, thinkers in Latin America such as <b>Raúl Prebisch</b> argued that poorer countries exporting raw materials were disadvantaged by trade with industrial countries. This is called <b>dependency theory</b>, and it influenced Wallerstein."],
      ["Praise and criticism", "⚖️", "The theory helps explain long links between places such as sugar islands, silver mines and factories. Critics say it can make periphery regions seem passive, over-stress Europe, and fit some cases (such as the rise of East Asian economies) badly."]],
      ["Think about which regions made goods and which supplied raw materials.", "The word semi means partly. Where does a semi-periphery sit?", "Example: cotton grown in India or Egypt feeding mills in Lancashire shows links between a periphery and a core. Now do yours."],
      [["Who wrote The Modern World-System (1974)?", "Immanuel Wallerstein", "Fernand Braudel|Edward Said"],
       ["In his model, a wealthy region is part of the...", "Core", "Periphery|Frontier"],
       ["A region supplying raw goods and labor is part of the...", "Periphery", "Core|Capital"],
       ["Regions in between belong to the...", "Semi-periphery", "Hinterland|Archipelago"],
       ["Wallerstein dated the modern world economy from about the...", "1500s", "800s|1900s"],
       ["Raúl Prebisch is linked to...", "Dependency theory", "Gunpowder studies|Medieval law"],
       ["One criticism of world-systems theory is that it can make the periphery seem...", "Passive", "Too powerful always|Invisible on maps"],
       ["The rise of East Asian economies fits the theory...", "Poorly in some ways", "Perfectly with no questions|Not at all connected to trade"]]),
    M("Big History", [
      ["From the Big Bang to now", "🌌", "<b>Big History</b> tells the story of everything from the Big Bang (about 13.8 billion years ago) to today. <b>David Christian</b> began teaching such a course at Macquarie University in Australia around 1989 and wrote <i>Maps of Time</i> (2004)."],
      ["Thresholds", "🪜", "Big historians organize the story around 'thresholds' when new things appear: stars, new chemical elements, our Solar System and Earth (about 4.5 billion years ago), life, humans (our species about 300,000 years ago), farming, and the modern world with fossil fuels. Each stage builds on the one before."],
      ["Classroom and critique", "🏫", "The Big History Project (from 2011) brought it to schools with support from Bill Gates. Supporters say it connects science and history; critics say it can skate over local details, and some find its pattern of rising complexity too neat."]],
      ["Think about very long time scales, billions of years instead of centuries.", "Remember the order: universe, stars, Earth, life, humans, farming, modern times.", "Example: the Sun formed about 4.6 billion years ago, and so the Earth is slightly younger. Now do yours."],
      [["Big History begins with the...", "Big Bang", "Roman Empire|Invention of writing"],
       ["The universe is about how old?", "13.8 billion years", "13.8 thousand years|138 million years"],
       ["Who wrote Maps of Time (2004)?", "David Christian", "Hayden White|Fernand Braudel"],
       ["Earth formed about...", "4.5 billion years ago", "4.5 million years ago|450 years ago"],
       ["Which came first?", "Stars", "Farming|Writing"],
       ["Homo sapiens appeared about...", "300,000 years ago", "3 million years ago|3,000 years ago"],
       ["In Big History, 'thresholds' are moments when...", "New kinds of complexity appear", "Wars end|Calendars change"],
       ["A criticism of Big History is that it can...", "Skate over local detail", "Ignore science|Skip the Earth"]]),
    M("Quantitative history and cliometrics", [
      ["Counting the past", "📊", "<b>Cliometrics</b> (named for Clio, the muse of history) applies economics and statistics to the past. <b>Robert Fogel</b> and <b>Douglass North</b> shared the 1993 Nobel Prize in Economics for this work. Historians use census data, tax records, ship logs and price lists to measure growth, wages or trade."],
      ["Strengths and fights", "⚔️", "Numbers can reveal patterns across many cases and test claims, such as how much railways really added to growth. A famous study, <i>Time on the Cross</i> (1974) by Fogel and Engerman, used data to argue about the economics of American slavery. It was fiercely criticized for its methods and for how it handled human suffering and the voices of enslaved people."],
      ["Using numbers well", "🧮", "Sources can be incomplete or inconsistent, so historians ask who collected the numbers and why. Good practice combines data with letters, diaries and other sources, and is clear about uncertainty."]],
      ["Think about what numbers can show and what they might miss.", "Remember the prize year and the idea of testing claims with data.", "Example: census records can show how many people moved to cities, but diaries show how they felt. Now do yours."],
      [["Cliometrics applies what to history?", "Economics and statistics", "Poetry and art|Cooking and sewing"],
       ["Clio is the muse of...", "History", "Music|War"],
       ["Fogel and North shared a Nobel Prize in...", "1993", "1893|1953"],
       ["Which source could help measure wages?", "Payroll and price records", "Fairy tales|Cartoons only"],
       ["Time on the Cross (1974) was...", "Fiercely criticized", "Universally praised|Never published"],
       ["A problem with old numbers is that they can be...", "Incomplete or inconsistent", "Too colorful|Too small to print"],
       ["Historians should ask who collected the numbers and...", "Why", "How fast|On which day of the week"],
       ["Good practice combines data with...", "Letters, diaries and other sources", "Only more data|Rumors"]]),
    M("Intellectual history and the history of concepts", [
      ["Ideas in context", "💭", "<b>Intellectual history</b> studies how people thought. <b>Quentin Skinner</b> and the 'Cambridge School' argued that to understand a text you must ask what the author was <i>doing</i> in his own time: answering rivals, making an argument, responding to events."],
      ["Words change", "🔤", "<b>Reinhart Koselleck</b> and others studied how basic concepts change meaning over time (<i>Begriffsgeschichte</i>, 'conceptual history'). 'Revolution' once meant a turn in an orbit, like a planet revolving, before it came to mean a fundamental change in politics. Words like liberty, democracy or nation have also changed."],
      ["A caution", "⚠️", "Reading old words with modern meanings is a mistake called <b>presentism</b>. Arthur Lovejoy's 1936 <i>Great Chain of Being</i> traced 'unit-ideas' across centuries, but later scholars worried that this ignores context."]],
      ["Think about meaning in the author's own time, not ours.", "A concept's meaning can shift. Compare the old and the new meaning.", "Example: the word 'nice' once meant foolish or simple in English. Meanings drift. Now do yours."],
      [["Intellectual history studies how people...", "Thought", "Traveled only|Fought only"],
       ["Quentin Skinner is associated with the...", "Cambridge School", "Annales school|Frankfurt School only"],
       ["Skinner urged asking what an author was...", "Doing in his own time", "Wearing|Eating"],
       ["Begriffsgeschichte means...", "Conceptual history", "Military history|Art history"],
       ["Reinhart Koselleck studied how concepts...", "Change meaning over time", "Never change|Were invented in 2000"],
       ["The word 'revolution' once referred to...", "A turn or orbit", "A harvest|A treaty"],
       ["Reading old words with modern meanings is...", "Presentism", "Genealogy|Prosopography"],
       ["Lovejoy's Great Chain of Being traced...", "Ideas across centuries", "Rivers of Asia|Roman roads"]]),
    M("Cultural history and thick description", [
      ["Meaning in everyday things", "🎭", "<b>Cultural history</b> studies symbols, rituals, stories and meanings. Anthropologist <b>Clifford Geertz</b> proposed <b>thick description</b>: describing not just what people do but what their actions mean to them. He borrowed Gilbert Ryle's example of the difference between a twitch and a deliberate wink."],
      ["A village mystery", "🏘️", "<b>Natalie Zemon Davis</b> wrote <i>The Return of Martin Guerre</i> (1983) about a sixteenth-century French village where a man claimed to be a returned husband. She used court records, then imagined carefully what people may have thought, and said where she was guessing."],
      ["Strengths and limits", "🔍", "Cultural history can recover how ordinary people saw their world. Critics say it can read too much into one ritual or story. Good cultural historians compare many sources and are open about what remains uncertain."]],
      ["Think about meanings behind actions, not just the actions themselves.", "The wink and the twitch look alike but mean different things.", "Example: handshakes and bows both greet people, but each carries different meanings in different places. Now do yours."],
      [["Thick description records what people do and what it...", "Means to them", "Costs to build|Weighs"],
       ["Thick description was proposed by...", "Clifford Geertz", "Leopold von Ranke|Thucydides"],
       ["The wink and the twitch show that similar actions can have different...", "Meanings", "Weights|Prices"],
       ["Natalie Zemon Davis wrote about...", "Martin Guerre", "Julius Caesar|Captain Cook"],
       ["The Return of Martin Guerre is set in...", "Sixteenth-century France", "Ancient Egypt|Modern Japan"],
       ["Davis used court records and was open about where she was...", "Guessing", "Hiding sources|Skipping the story"],
       ["Cultural history studies symbols, rituals and...", "Meanings", "Only taxes|Only battles"],
       ["A criticism of cultural history is that it can...", "Read too much into one ritual", "Use too many numbers|Avoid people"]]),
    M("The history of emotions", [
      ["Feelings have a past", "😊", "Historians ask whether and how emotions change over time. Lucien Febvre called in 1941 for a history of sensibility. <b>Norbert Elias</b>, in <i>The Civilizing Process</i> (1939), argued that European manners and self-control changed over the centuries, from the Middle Ages to modern times."],
      ["Emotional communities", "👥", "<b>Barbara Rosenwein</b> proposed 'emotional communities': groups, such as monasteries or royal courts, that share rules about which feelings are good, how to show them and what to value. <b>William Reddy</b> studied how emotional language is used to shape what people feel."],
      ["Sources and caution", "🧭", "Historians study letters, diaries, sermons, laws and art. They ask whether people's words show private feelings or social expectations. They also avoid assuming that people long ago felt exactly as we do."]],
      ["Think about feelings being shaped by groups, words and rules.", "Remember the idea of a community with its own emotional rules.", "Example: in some times and places, public weeping at funerals was expected; in others, it was discouraged. Now do yours."],
      [["The history of emotions asks how feelings...", "Change over time", "Stay the same always|Show up on maps"],
       ["Norbert Elias wrote The Civilizing...", "Process", "Empire|Treaty"],
       ["Elias studied changes in manners and...", "Self-control", "Calendars|Taxes"],
       ["Barbara Rosenwein proposed emotional...", "Communities", "Revolutions|Treaties"],
       ["An emotional community shares rules about...", "Which feelings to show and value", "Which roads to build|Which crops to plant"],
       ["Sources for the history of emotions include...", "Letters, diaries and sermons", "Only maps|Only coins"],
       ["Historians must beware of assuming that people long ago...", "Felt exactly as we do", "Had any feelings|Wrote anything"],
       ["Emotional words can show private feelings or...", "Social expectations", "Weather reports|Taxes"]]),
    M("Writing African history", [
      ["A challenge to old ideas", "🌍", "In the colonial era, some European writers claimed that Africa had no history before outsiders arrived. They ignored African states, trade and traditions. After independence, African and other scholars worked to correct this, using new methods."],
      ["Many kinds of sources", "🧩", "<b>Jan Vansina</b> showed in <i>Oral Tradition</i> (1961) how to test spoken histories. Historians also use linguistics, archaeology, Arabic manuscripts (for example from Timbuktu), travel accounts and colonial records. Scholars at Ibadan, such as <b>Kenneth Dike</b>, wrote influential histories from African sources."],
      ["A shared project", "📚", "<b>Joseph Ki-Zerbo</b> of Burkina Faso helped edit UNESCO's eight-volume <i>General History of Africa</i>, begun in the 1960s and completed in 1999. <b>Cheikh Anta Diop</b> of Senegal argued for the importance of Egypt and Africa's ancient contributions, and his claims were widely debated."]],
      ["Think about how different kinds of evidence can be combined.", "Remember the names of scholars and the places they worked.", "Example: a king-list from oral tradition can be compared with a visitor's written account and with archaeology. Now do yours."],
      [["The old claim that Africa had no history was...", "Mistaken", "Proven by archives|Accepted by all scholars"],
       ["Who wrote Oral Tradition (1961)?", "Jan Vansina", "Karl Marx|Herodotus"],
       ["Oral traditions can be tested by comparing them with...", "Other kinds of evidence", "Nothing at all|Random guesses"],
       ["Which kind of source helps historians of West Africa?", "Arabic manuscripts", "Radio shows|Film posters"],
       ["Ki-Zerbo was from...", "Burkina Faso", "Brazil|Iceland"],
       ["UNESCO's General History of Africa was completed in...", "1999", "1799|1899"],
       ["Kenneth Dike was associated with the university of...", "Ibadan", "Oxford only|Nairobi only"],
       ["Which Senegalese scholar's claims about ancient Egypt were widely debated?", "Cheikh Anta Diop", "Jan Vansina|Barbara Rosenwein"]]),
    M("Causation and explanation in history", [
      ["Why did it happen?", "🔗", "Historians distinguish causes. A <b>necessary</b> cause must be present for an event, though alone it may not be enough. A <b>sufficient</b> cause is enough to bring it about. Most big events have many causes, some <b>long-term</b> and some <b>triggers</b>."],
      ["Laws or stories?", "⚖️", "<b>Carl Hempel</b> (1942) argued that historical explanations should work like science, with general laws. Many historians replied that events are unique and need explanations that fit their context. <b>Max Weber</b> used 'ideal types' (simplified models) to compare cases."],
      ["Careful reasoning", "⚠️", "Beware the fallacy <b>post hoc ergo propter hoc</b>: 'after this, therefore because of this'. An event that follows another did not necessarily result from it. Historians weigh causes, test them against evidence, and say how confident they are."]],
      ["Think about whether the cause had to be there or was enough on its own.", "Remember that something happening after another thing does not prove cause.", "Example: a spark may start a fire, but dry grass is also needed. Both matter, in different ways. Now do yours."],
      [["A cause that must be present for an event is...", "Necessary", "Sufficient|Accidental"],
       ["A cause strong enough on its own is...", "Sufficient", "Necessary|Optional"],
       ["A trigger is usually...", "A short-term event that sets things off", "A treaty from centuries before|A geographic feature"],
       ["Carl Hempel argued explanations should use...", "General laws", "Only poems|Only dates"],
       ["Max Weber used 'ideal types', which are...", "Simplified models for comparison", "Real portraits|Lists of kings"],
       ["Post hoc ergo propter hoc means...", "After this, therefore because of this", "Before all things|Out of many one"],
       ["This is a fallacy because...", "Coming later does not prove causing", "Time never passes|Causes are not real"],
       ["Good historians say how...", "Confident they are", "Fast they wrote|Loud they spoke"]]),
    M("Objectivity and bias in history", [
      ["A noble dream?", "🕊️", "In <i>That Noble Dream</i> (1988), <b>Peter Novick</b> traced the history of the American historians' ideal of objectivity. Many early professionals believed they could tell the past 'as it was'. Later challenges asked whether any writer can be free of their time, class, gender, nation or beliefs."],
      ["Positions and standpoints", "📍", "Scholars now often speak of <b>positionality</b>: where a historian stands may shape what they ask and see. New voices, such as women, workers and colonized peoples, have raised fresh questions and found new sources, which has improved history rather than ruined it."],
      ["Fairness as practice", "✅", "Many historians treat objectivity as a goal rather than a guarantee. They try to be fair to other views, to be open about their sources and assumptions, to let evidence change their minds, and to let others check their work."]],
      ["Think about how someone's background may shape their questions.", "Fairness can be practiced even if perfect objectivity is impossible.", "Example: a judge can be fair by listening to both sides and explaining the reasons, even though no judge is perfectly neutral. Now do yours."],
      [["Who wrote That Noble Dream (1988)?", "Peter Novick", "Hayden White|Jan Vansina"],
       ["The 'noble dream' was the ideal of...", "Objectivity", "Empire|Revolution"],
       ["Positionality means...", "Where a historian stands may shape their view", "Being on a map|A battle formation"],
       ["New voices in history have often...", "Raised fresh questions and found new sources", "Made history impossible|Ended research"],
       ["Many historians treat objectivity as a...", "Goal", "Law of physics|Joke"],
       ["A good practice for fairness is to be open about...", "Sources and assumptions", "Nothing|Only the conclusion"],
       ["Fairness includes being willing to...", "Let evidence change your mind", "Ignore other views|Hide documents"],
       ["Allowing others to check your work helps make history...", "More reliable", "Less public|Shorter"]]),
    M("Comparing revolutions: Brinton and Skocpol", [
      ["Looking for patterns", "🔥", "<b>Crane Brinton</b> compared the English, American, French and Russian revolutions in <i>The Anatomy of Revolution</i> (1938). He described stages: a moderate phase, then a radical phase, then a calmer return. He used the image of a fever."],
      ["A structural view", "🏗️", "<b>Theda Skocpol</b>, in <i>States and Social Revolutions</i> (1979), compared France (1789), Russia (1917) and China (1911-1949). She argued revolutions begin when states under heavy military and financial strain lose control, at a time when peasants are able to rebel. The outcome often is a stronger state."],
      ["Questions of method", "🧪", "Comparing only a few cases can mislead, and revolutions differ. Critics say Skocpol undervalued ideas and leaders. Comparison is still a powerful tool: it helps ask why similar crises led to different results."]],
      ["Think about what each author compared and what they thought mattered most.", "Skocpol stressed states and structures, not just ideas or leaders.", "Example: comparing two droughts shows that one led to famine and one did not, which makes you ask why. Now do yours."],
      [["Crane Brinton wrote The Anatomy of...", "Revolution", "Empire|Nations"],
       ["Brinton used the image of a...", "Fever", "River|Mountain"],
       ["Who wrote States and Social Revolutions (1979)?", "Theda Skocpol", "Crane Brinton|Immanuel Wallerstein"],
       ["Skocpol compared France, Russia and...", "China", "Brazil|Egypt"],
       ["Skocpol stressed that revolutions need states that are...", "Under heavy strain", "Perfectly stable|Absent forever"],
       ["Revolutions often end up with a...", "Stronger state", "Smaller map|Different alphabet always"],
       ["Comparing only a few cases can...", "Mislead", "Prove everything|End all debate"],
       ["A criticism of Skocpol is that she undervalued...", "Ideas and leaders", "Geography entirely|Dates"]]),
    M("The frontier thesis and its critics", [
      ["Turner's idea", "🤠", "In 1893 <b>Frederick Jackson Turner</b> presented 'The Significance of the Frontier in American History' at a meeting in Chicago. He argued that the westward-moving frontier shaped American democracy, individualism and optimism."],
      ["The critics", "🗺️", "Later historians said the thesis treated land as empty when Native nations lived there, ignored women, Mexicans, Chinese workers and others, and overlooked the role of government, railways and big business. Patricia Limerick's <i>The Legacy of Conquest</i> (1987), part of the 'New Western History', stressed conquest and continuing connections."],
      ["The middle ground", "🤝", "<b>Richard White</b>'s <i>The Middle Ground</i> (1991) showed how, in the Great Lakes region, Native peoples and Europeans developed shared customs of diplomacy and trade. Historians also compare frontiers elsewhere, such as Russian expansion into Siberia."]],
      ["Think about whose viewpoint is left out.", "Recall the year and the place of the meeting where Turner spoke.", "Example: a map that shows only roads and towns may leave out the people who were already living there. Now do yours."],
      [["Who proposed the frontier thesis in 1893?", "Frederick Jackson Turner", "Patricia Limerick|Richard White"],
       ["Turner said the frontier shaped American...", "Democracy and individualism", "Monarchy|Feudalism"],
       ["A major criticism is that Turner treated the land as...", "Empty", "Overcrowded|Frozen"],
       ["The New Western History stressed...", "Conquest and continuing connections", "Only cowboys|Only gold"],
       ["Who wrote The Legacy of Conquest (1987)?", "Patricia Limerick", "Frederick Jackson Turner|Crane Brinton"],
       ["Richard White's The Middle Ground studied...", "Native-European shared customs", "Roman roads|Ancient Greece"],
       ["The Middle Ground focuses on the region of the...", "Great Lakes", "Gulf of Mexico|Pacific coast"],
       ["Historians also compare the American frontier with expansion into...", "Siberia", "Antarctica|The Moon"]]),
    M("Late industrialization", [
      ["Latecomers", "🏭", "Britain industrialized first. <b>Alexander Gerschenkron</b>, in <i>Economic Backwardness in Historical Perspective</i> (1962), argued that countries that industrialized later followed different paths. They could copy technology but needed ways to gather money fast."],
      ["Banks, states and plans", "🏦", "In Germany, big banks helped finance heavy industry. In Russia, where private capital was thin, the state played a big role, building railways and factories under Count Witte in the 1890s. Japan's Meiji government set up model factories and sold many to private firms."],
      ["Testing the idea", "🧪", "Critics note that not all latecomers followed the pattern and that 'backwardness' can be an unfair label. The idea is still useful for comparing how Germany, Russia, Japan and, later, South Korea or China developed."]],
      ["Think about which institution gathered money in each country.", "Latecomers could borrow technology. What else did they need?", "Example: a runner who starts late may use a shortcut, but needs help to catch up. Now do yours."],
      [["Which country industrialized first?", "Britain", "Russia|Japan"],
       ["Who wrote Economic Backwardness in Historical Perspective?", "Alexander Gerschenkron", "Theda Skocpol|Peter Novick"],
       ["Latecomers could copy...", "Technology", "Nothing|Only foods"],
       ["In Germany, heavy industry was financed by big...", "Banks", "Monasteries|Guilds"],
       ["In Russia, a major role was played by the...", "State", "Pirates|Guilds only"],
       ["Count Witte promoted railways in the...", "1890s", "1690s|1990s"],
       ["Japan's Meiji government set up...", "Model factories", "Medieval castles|Slave ships"],
       ["Critics say the word 'backwardness' can be...", "An unfair label", "Perfectly neutral always|A kind of tax"]]),
    M("Histories of the sea", [
      ["Seas as stages", "🌊", "<b>Fernand Braudel</b>'s <i>The Mediterranean and the World of Philip II</i> (1949) treated a sea as a historical subject, with slow geography, medium-term economic cycles and quick events. <b>Peregrine Horden</b> and <b>Nicholas Purcell</b>, in <i>The Corrupting Sea</i> (2000), stressed the Mediterranean's many small regions connected by risk, trade and sailing."],
      ["Oceans in motion", "⛵", "Historians also write about the Indian Ocean, the Atlantic and the Pacific. <b>Epeli Hau'ofa</b>, a Tongan-Fijian writer, argued in 'Our Sea of Islands' (1994) that Oceania is not small, isolated dots but a vast, connected sea of voyaging peoples."],
      ["Why it matters", "🧭", "Sea-centered history crosses modern borders and shows how people, goods and diseases moved. Critics warn that it can overlook the land and that every sea is different."]],
      ["Think about the sea as the main character in the story.", "Match the author with the sea or region that interested him.", "Example: Hanseatic towns on the Baltic and North Seas are another case of a shared sea-world. Now do yours."],
      [["Braudel wrote about the...", "Mediterranean", "Caspian|Red Sea only"],
       ["Braudel used slow geography, medium-term cycles and...", "Quick events", "No events|Only poems"],
       ["Horden and Purcell wrote The...", "Corrupting Sea", "Silent Sea|Golden Sea"],
       ["They stressed the Mediterranean's many connected...", "Small regions", "Empires of ice|Skyscrapers"],
       ["Epeli Hau'ofa wrote about...", "Oceania", "The Arctic|Central Asia"],
       ["Hau'ofa called Oceania a sea of...", "Islands", "Deserts|Glaciers"],
       ["Sea-centered history crosses...", "Modern borders", "Time itself|Oceans of lava"],
       ["A warning about sea-centered history is that it may overlook the...", "Land", "Ships|Water"]]),
    M("The history of childhood and the family", [
      ["Was childhood invented?", "🧒", "In <i>Centuries of Childhood</i> (1960), <b>Philippe Ariès</b> claimed that medieval society had no real idea of childhood, and that it developed in early modern Europe. Later historians such as Linda Pollock and Shulamith Shahar found evidence that medieval parents loved and cared for children."],
      ["Sources and change", "🧸", "Historians study toys, schoolbooks, portraits, diaries and court records. They show that ideas about childhood, schooling and work changed. In the 1800s many countries passed laws limiting child labor and later required schooling. The United Nations adopted the Convention on the Rights of the Child in 1989."],
      ["Families in history", "👪", "Family history asks about household size, marriage age, inheritance and who cared for whom. It varies widely: extended families were common in many places, and household patterns differed between regions."]],
      ["Think about what a good historian does when a claim seems too bold.", "Remember the kinds of sources that show how children lived.", "Example: a medieval grave with a small toy suggests that children were mourned. Now do yours."],
      [["Who wrote Centuries of Childhood (1960)?", "Philippe Ariès", "Norbert Elias|Edward Said"],
       ["Ariès claimed that medieval society lacked an idea of...", "Childhood", "Farming|Religion"],
       ["Later historians found that medieval parents...", "Loved and cared for children", "Never saw children|Had no families"],
       ["Which is a source for the history of childhood?", "Toys and schoolbooks", "Satellite photos|Airplane logs"],
       ["In the 1800s many countries passed laws limiting...", "Child labor", "Schooling|Libraries"],
       ["The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child came in...", "1989", "1789|1939"],
       ["Family history asks about marriage age, inheritance and...", "Household size", "Moon phases|Ocean tides"],
       ["Households were...", "Different in different regions", "Identical everywhere|Never studied"]]),
    M("The Needham question", [
      ["A famous puzzle", "❓", "<b>Joseph Needham</b> (1900-1995), a British biochemist, began <i>Science and Civilisation in China</i> in 1954, a multi-volume work on Chinese science and technology. He asked: why did modern science arise in Europe, not China, even though China had led in many techniques such as paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass?"],
      ["Many answers", "💡", "Scholars have suggested differences in institutions, politics, markets, religion, language or education. Some say China's civil-service exams shaped learning toward the classics; others say Europe's competing states and universities encouraged experiments. No single answer is accepted."],
      ["Questioning the question", "🔄", "Critics ask whether the question assumes that European science is the standard, and whether 'modern science' arose suddenly. Historians now stress exchange: Islamic science, Indian mathematics, and Jesuit visits to China all linked traditions."]],
      ["Think about what Needham was trying to explain.", "Remember that many answers have been offered and none is final.", "Example: the printing press existed in both China and Europe, but its effects differed in each place. Now do yours."],
      [["Joseph Needham was a British...", "Biochemist", "General|King"],
       ["Needham began Science and Civilisation in China in...", "1954", "1654|1854"],
       ["Which invention was developed early in China?", "Printing", "Telescope|Steam locomotive"],
       ["Which was also developed early in China?", "Gunpowder", "Telephone|Radio"],
       ["The Needham question asks why modern science arose in...", "Europe rather than China", "Antarctica|Only Egypt"],
       ["How many accepted answers are there?", "No single accepted answer", "Exactly one|Exactly two"],
       ["A critic might say the question assumes that...", "European science is the standard", "China had no history|Science is not real"],
       ["Historians now stress that science traditions...", "Exchanged ideas", "Never met|Were identical"]]),
    M("Biography and prosopography", [
      ["One life, or many", "🧑‍🎓", "<b>Biography</b> tells the story of one person's life and times. <b>Prosopography</b> studies a group by collecting facts about many individuals' lives, such as birth, family, career and marriages, to see patterns."],
      ["Famous examples", "📚", "<b>Lewis Namier</b> studied members of the British House of Commons in the 1760s and argued that personal ties and interests mattered more than party ideology (1929). <b>Ronald Syme</b>, in <i>The Roman Revolution</i> (1939), used the family and political links of Roman elites to explain the rise of Augustus."],
      ["Strengths and limits", "⚖️", "These methods rely on lists, records and letters, which survive mostly for elite people. Digital databases now help scholars handle thousands of lives. Historians also use biography to illuminate ordinary people, but few sources survive for them."]],
      ["Think about whether a question is about one life or a whole group.", "Prosopography collects facts about many people to find patterns.", "Example: a study of 200 craft workers' marriage records might show who married within the same trade. Now do yours."],
      [["A biography focuses on...", "One person's life", "A whole continent|A single year"],
       ["Prosopography collects facts about...", "A group of individuals", "One building|A coin"],
       ["Lewis Namier studied members of the...", "British House of Commons", "Roman Senate|French Estates"],
       ["Namier stressed personal ties and...", "Interests", "Foods|Fashion"],
       ["Ronald Syme wrote The Roman...", "Revolution", "Republic|Games"],
       ["Syme's book helped explain the rise of...", "Augustus", "Charlemagne|Napoleon"],
       ["A limit of these methods is that records mostly survive for...", "Elite people", "Everyone equally|Animals"],
       ["Digital databases help scholars handle...", "Thousands of lives", "Only one letter|No data"]]),
  ];
